WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.480
Are you afraid of haunted houses?&nbsp;
What about haunted castles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:11.360
or prisons, or mental asylums? We’ve all seen or&nbsp;
heard tales of terrible, tortured souls walking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:17.360
down long and lonely hallways, looking for&nbsp;
people to pass their ancient pain onto. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:24.960
what about something a lot more subtle. Perhaps as&nbsp;
humble and unassuming as a cozy little woodshed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:32.000
An adventurer named Cait would find that,&nbsp;
sometimes, terrible things hide in simple places.&nbsp;

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.960
With every step, twigs broke under the sole&nbsp;
of her boot, their snaps echoing around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.720
emptiness of the surrounding forest. Birds&nbsp;
cawed down at her from their perches up above,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:48.800
a grave omen, a warning not to tread further…&nbsp;
well, at least to anyone who actually bought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:54.320
into all that superstitious stuff. Cait was far&nbsp;
from a believer in that sort of thing, although,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.320 --> 00:01:00.480
at the same time, she had more than a passing&nbsp;
interest in all things occult and unnatural.&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:05.520
For her, it was less about the actual practice&nbsp;
of certain things, the appeal was more about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:12.160
the stories that those practices inspired.&nbsp;
Legends and myths, spooky tales of ghosts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:18.080
or the dead rising from their graves and,&nbsp;
of course, the magical mother of them all:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:24.160
witchcraft. Although her fascination with&nbsp;
the inexplicable and macabre rarely, if ever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:30.080
overlapped with her other big passion: hiking.
Until, of course, today.&nbsp;

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:34.640
She could trek anywhere and wanted to aim&nbsp;
for everywhere. Through sprawling national&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:39.520
parks filled with vibrant greenery, up the&nbsp;
sides of mountains climbing up to the peaks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:45.120
to across deserts, following the almost empty&nbsp;
highways to get from one tiny rural town to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:49.280
next. There was something about getting&nbsp;
to not just know about the world but to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:57.120
actively go and see it with her own two eyes, to&nbsp;
actually be out there in it, that felt so right.&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.920
On her current journey, Cait had taken herself&nbsp;
on a long trail through the heart of the American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:07.200
Midwest. Lugging a heavy pack over her shoulders,&nbsp;
filled with her tent, sleeping bag, outdoor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:12.960
cooking supplies, and all manner of camping gear,&nbsp;
she had set off following the winding path of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:19.680
Patoka River, which ran around or adjacent to a&nbsp;
few of the major cities in the state of Indiana.&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:23.920
Cait was hoping to follow the flow of its&nbsp;
main body of water all the way through to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:29.440
the city of Jasper. From there, it would be&nbsp;
a straight shot over to the similarly named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:33.520
Patoka Lake, and after she had walked&nbsp;
around the entire circumference of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:39.120
it would be a long hike East towards West&nbsp;
Virginia, home of the Blue Ridge Mountains. Sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.760
it was one hell of an ambitious trip for one&nbsp;
person traveling alone, but Cait liked to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:48.560
keep her journeys unencumbered; having someone&nbsp;
else coming with her would just slow her down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:53.040
or they wouldn’t be able to keep up with&nbsp;
her at all. There’s no fun at all in that.&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.360
Stopping for the night, Cait lit herself a&nbsp;
campfire. She had to make sure to keep it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:02.560
far enough from the trees to avoid starting any&nbsp;
potential catastrophes, but also not too close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:07.360
to the wet bank of the Patoka, otherwise, her&nbsp;
kindling wouldn’t catch alight. There were so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:12.240
variables to consider for any aspiring camper.&nbsp;
Sparks from the flint that she always took on her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:17.920
travels struck the dry twigs she had collected,&nbsp;
and soon, a gentle orange glow and wave of low&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:23.280
heat were radiating from it. The fire provided&nbsp;
Cait with just enough light to unfold and peg down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:29.040
her tent as the diminishing visibility of the dusk&nbsp;
gave way to the encroaching nighttime darkness.&nbsp;

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:34.000
Before getting comfortable in her sleeping bag,&nbsp;
she put out her campfire using water from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:39.600
Patoka, then zipped up the front of her tent,&nbsp;
keeping a flimsy cloth barrier between herself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:44.560
and the local wildlife. Not everyone found&nbsp;
it as easy to sleep in the woods as Cait did.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:49.360
She couldn’t remember a time when the noise of&nbsp;
breaking twigs or the sounds of movement outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:54.720
had ever kept her awake. Even now, the rustle of&nbsp;
leaves and branches did little to disturb her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:59.440
regardless of whether it was just the wind…&nbsp;
or something else moving through the bushes.&nbsp;

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:04.560
Woken up by the sun rising, its warm light&nbsp;
breaking through the trees, Cait readied herself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:09.360
to leave. She never stopped at the same place&nbsp;
for more than one night, and rarely slept for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:14.960
any longer than she needed to. A nice, eight-hour&nbsp;
recharge of her energy reserves, and then it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:20.640
back to the open road. After a quick breakfast,&nbsp;
she packed up her single-person campsite, folded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:25.440
up her tent, and stuffed it back into her bag.
Cait had been just about to leave when she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:30.160
noticed something was missing. Her compass, it&nbsp;
had been in her backpack, which she kept in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:34.880
tent with her all night. Quickly scanning the&nbsp;
ground around her, she couldn’t see it. Weird,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:38.800
but she figured she had obviously dropped it&nbsp;
at some point during her hike and would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:43.520
to pick up a new one when she got to Jasper. It&nbsp;
didn’t matter, she could just follow the Patoka&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:48.240
River as planned, although it didn’t quite sit&nbsp;
easily with her the rest of the day’s trek.&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:53.840
She’d packed the compass, that she was absolutely&nbsp;
certain of; after all, she knew it wasn’t like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.840 --> 00:05:00.000
her to leave it behind. But then again, had&nbsp;
she… could she have forgotten it entirely?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.560
Over the next few hours, the question went round&nbsp;
and round in Cait’s head. As she walked along the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:09.600
bank of the river, silently starting to doubt&nbsp;
herself, she drifted a little deeper into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:15.920
woods until, out of the corner of her eye, she&nbsp;
spotted something standing among the trees.&nbsp;

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:21.840
It wasn’t an animal or even a figure, but a&nbsp;
structure. Spending so much time out in nature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.640
anything man-made usually stuck out as an&nbsp;
obvious, unignorable eyesore to Cait. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:32.400
looked at it from a distance; it looked like an&nbsp;
old house, isolated out in the woods, a short walk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:37.440
from the Patoka River. Checking the sky over her&nbsp;
shoulder, Cait noticed it was getting late again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:42.400
the daylight having gradually lowered while she’d&nbsp;
been wondering about why she had left her compass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:48.240
behind… or if it had been taken. Deciding&nbsp;
to set up camp closer to the little house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:54.000
she turned and headed in to inspect it closer.
The place was far from the lap of modern luxury.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:58.880
Not that Cait couldn’t live without running&nbsp;
water or electricity for long stints of time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:03.760
But it wasn’t a house that had been built&nbsp;
recently. Although she was no expert in either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:08.480
archaeology or architecture, Cait didn’t&nbsp;
think anyone had lived there for decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:15.120
possibly even centuries. It was almost medieval,&nbsp;
a single room that was completely ramshackle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:20.560
practically falling apart at the seams. Everywhere&nbsp;
she looked, the wood that had originally been used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:25.520
to build it was either rotting away or had&nbsp;
been broken into splintered remains. Plants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:29.760
from the forest had started growing through the&nbsp;
floors and the walls as if nature was trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:34.800
reclaim this spot of land where someone had once&nbsp;
come to get away from the rest of the world.&nbsp;

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:39.680
Sighing, Cait shook her head as she looked&nbsp;
at the dilapidated house; there was no way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:44.320
even she could sleep there. It was too much&nbsp;
of a safety risk, parts of the ceiling could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:48.640
easily fall on her during the night if the wind&nbsp;
picked up enough. Turning to leave, she stopped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:55.280
in her tracks as she noticed another structure,&nbsp;
much smaller, behind the house. It had been so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.280 --> 00:07:00.560
dwarfed in size by the slightly bigger building&nbsp;
that it was completely obscured from view. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:06.240
it looked to be in much better shape in terms&nbsp;
of its structural integrity. It was a woodshed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:11.600
its front door hanging lazily open. While not&nbsp;
exactly brand new by anyone’s standards, it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:17.360
at least far more intact than the main house.
Taking a few steps forward, Cait felt something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:22.320
glass crunch under the heel of her boot. She&nbsp;
scanned the floor, instinctively checking for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:27.920
any more so as not to injure herself. Even with&nbsp;
the low amount of sunlight fading ever rapidly, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:32.960
didn’t look like more. Kicking her boot to loosen&nbsp;
the shards, she made her way towards the woodshed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:36.960
not noticing that what she’d been&nbsp;
standing on had been a compass.&nbsp;

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:42.400
Reaching the shed, she peered inside. It was pitch&nbsp;
black within, almost as dark as the rest of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:47.440
forest would be in a few minutes. Taking off her&nbsp;
backpack and setting it down, she searched inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:52.320
for her flashlight. Retrieving it and flipping&nbsp;
the on switch, she looked back into the shed…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:57.280
and that was when she saw it. Taking a step back,&nbsp;
Cait tripped over her bag, scrabbling back up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:02.480
to her feet to make sure she had seen what she&nbsp;
thought she had. It was just lying there, still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:07.440
and lifeless. For a split second, she had hoped&nbsp;
it was a trick of the light, just an old, decaying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:13.600
lump of wood. But it wasn’t… it was a body.
The remains were charred, reduced to little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:18.800
more than bones some time ago. Even the ground&nbsp;
next to it looked like it had been scorched,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:24.800
blackened by some intense fire damage. Cait’s eyes&nbsp;
were so transfixed on the burned mass of bones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:30.480
that a sudden sound startled her. Thunder growled&nbsp;
above, immediately telling her she had little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:34.960
choice in what to do next. Her options were she&nbsp;
could either sleep outdoors and get drenched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:40.480
by the rain, and possibly catch hypothermia from&nbsp;
being cold and wet. Or she could spend a night in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:47.440
the cramped confines of this woodshed, which she&nbsp;
would have to share with a long dead body. Walking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:52.640
back outside to retrieve her bag, she pulled out&nbsp;
a groundsheet and used it to cover up the bones.&nbsp;

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:58.800
It was a mostly sleepless night, and those were a&nbsp;
rarity for Cait. Any sleep she did get was light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:03.840
easily interrupted by as little as her own&nbsp;
movements, and offered her no actual rest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:08.800
But for the next few hours, she just lay awake&nbsp;
on the wooden floor. It wasn’t the sounds of rain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:13.680
drumming against the shed’s ceiling or the booming&nbsp;
of thunderclaps that were keeping her awake.&nbsp;

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:18.720
Thoughts of what she was going to do about the&nbsp;
bones kept coming to the forefront of Cait’s mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:23.440
should she report it to the police when she got&nbsp;
to Jasper? Was there even any point if she had no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:28.960
idea where on the map this abandoned place was?&nbsp;
Frustrated, she tossed and turned, eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:35.440
sitting up as something made a sound. It was a&nbsp;
low creaking, rusty metal hinge screeching for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:41.360
lack of any oiling over the years. The door was&nbsp;
open, swinging lazily in the decreasing winds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:45.520
its lock having long since broken.
Grumbling to herself, Cait reached for an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:50.400
old shovel that had been left in the shed and went&nbsp;
to grab the open door. As her head poked out from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:55.520
the shed, she looked around. It was the middle&nbsp;
of the night, nothing to be seen anywhere around.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.520 --> 00:10:00.160
Yet she couldn’t escape the feeling she&nbsp;
was being watched. Taking the shovel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:05.680
she jammed it underneath the doorframe, keeping&nbsp;
it closed. At last, there was quiet. Maybe now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:11.360
she’d be able to get some much needed sleep, and&nbsp;
she did… but that’s when the nightmare started.&nbsp;

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:17.680
The dream was bizarre, unlike any Cait had ever&nbsp;
experienced before. She instantly recognized the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:22.880
warm, flickering orange glow of a large fire&nbsp;
before her. Naturally, she started to pace her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:28.080
way towards it, the fire’s heat washing over&nbsp;
her the closer she got. But as she approached,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:33.200
Cait noticed something… moving amongst the&nbsp;
flames. Arms flailed, trying desperately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:39.600
to extinguish the blaze, legs kicking and&nbsp;
writhing. And that was on top of the screaming.&nbsp;

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:44.800
Another figure was standing facing the bonfire,&nbsp;
their back to Cait as she walked closer still.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:49.760
With every step, the sound of insects buzzing&nbsp;
could be heard, getting louder and louder. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:55.440
it wasn’t until she stepped near enough that she&nbsp;
saw where the noise was coming from. A swarm of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.440 --> 00:11:00.480
bugs was darting through the air, hovering around&nbsp;
the other figure; there were enough of the insects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:05.440
that they formed what looked like a cloak, every&nbsp;
part of it moving and buzzing of its own accord.&nbsp;

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:10.640
The whole nightmare was so vivid, Cait had no&nbsp;
idea if it had been brought on by her exhaustion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:16.000
or that her mind was still fixated on the&nbsp;
bones she’d found in the woodshed, dehydration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:20.720
lack of food, or even some other influence.&nbsp;
It could have been any one of those factors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:26.320
that had caused her to have a dream like this…&nbsp;
or a combination of all of them. Nonetheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:31.040
she could feel the bugs tapping at her skin&nbsp;
as she walked up to the other figure, still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:36.720
unable to see them clearly. The noise of insect&nbsp;
wings whizzed past her ears, louder than ever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:41.680
loud enough to be real. Putting a hand on the&nbsp;
figure’s shoulder, Cait turned them around.&nbsp;

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:48.240
It was a woman, or what was left of her. She had&nbsp;
huge, gaping wounds left by something that had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:50.546
been cruelly buried in her face, with similar&nbsp;
injuries lower down at her neck, and on her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.546 --> 00:11:52.160
chest. There were gaps in her skin, as if chunks&nbsp;
of her flesh had been hacked away. Yet somehow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:59.040
she was still standing, dead but also alive at the&nbsp;
same time. The swarm of bugs around her moved with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:04.560
her body as she turned and faced Cait, a pair of&nbsp;
glassy eyes staring at her. They were both blank,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:09.760
completely empty, each one missing its pupil.
Opening her mouth, the woman let out a piercing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:15.120
blood curdling shriek. All around her,&nbsp;
the sound of the bugs got louder, almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:20.880
deafening. And as she screamed… so did Cait.
Panting and shuddering, she sat bolt upright,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:26.640
shock wracking her whole body, heart thudding&nbsp;
against her ribcage. Instantly, the exact moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:31.680
she woke up, Cait realized that feeling was back.&nbsp;
She had spent so much of her time hiking through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:35.440
the woods on her own that she could tell&nbsp;
when someone was watching her. In the dark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:41.520
she fumbled for her flashlight, and clicked it&nbsp;
on to reveal… the door was still jammed shut.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:46.240
Apart from her, there was no one else&nbsp;
inside the shed… no one alive, at least.&nbsp;

00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:51.200
Looking over her shoulder, she could see the bones&nbsp;
behind her and immediately backed away from them;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:56.160
as far as she could in the tiny wooden hut.&nbsp;
She had covered them before going to bed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.160 --> 00:13:00.960
she was certain of it. Hadn’t she? All of&nbsp;
a sudden, Cait felt her skin start to itch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:05.360
an intense, irritating feeling. She had&nbsp;
experienced the effects of poison ivy and all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:10.320
sorts of plants that cause similar reactions,&nbsp;
but this was easily a thousand times worse.&nbsp;

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:15.120
Her skin felt like it was burning; she scraped&nbsp;
her fingernails down her exposed arm, trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:20.080
suppress the itching even for a moment but it&nbsp;
did nothing. She scratched at the other arm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:27.360
over and over again in one spot until her flesh&nbsp;
was pink and raw. Still no change. Cait raked her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:33.120
nails over her own body, this time leaving a deep&nbsp;
scratch trailing over her arm. Under the light of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:38.000
her torch, she realized that she’d drawn blood.
She looked back at the bones in the scorched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:42.160
corner of the shed. The skull was facing&nbsp;
straight towards her, hollowed-out eye&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:48.400
sockets staring as empty as the pupilless eyes&nbsp;
of the woman in her nightmare. It had done this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:52.880
it must have done, she thought. Reaching&nbsp;
towards the door, dropping her flashlight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.880 --> 00:13:57.120
she wrenched the shovel out from where&nbsp;
she’d used it to wedge the entrance closed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.120 --> 00:14:01.680
She stood up, kicking her sleeping bag and&nbsp;
backpack to the side. As hard as she could,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:05.120
desperate to make the itching stop,&nbsp;
Cait brought the shovel downwards.&nbsp;

00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:09.280
The head of it snapped the already weakened&nbsp;
floorboards of the shed, exposing the soft dry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:15.120
earth beneath. Maybe if she buried those charred&nbsp;
remains, whatever was happening to her would stop.&nbsp;

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:19.840
Frantically, she started digging, hurling&nbsp;
shovelfuls of dirt out through the shed door.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:25.600
It listed further open, creaking loudly on&nbsp;
its rusted hinges again, causing Cait to turn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:30.400
startled at the sound of it knocking against&nbsp;
the outer wall of the shed. Someone was looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:35.120
right at her, she could feel it as real&nbsp;
and as persistent as the itching feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.120 --> 00:14:40.960
that crawled over her skin like the tiny legs of&nbsp;
an army of insects. She dug further, eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:45.120
throwing the shovel to one side and dragging&nbsp;
her hands through the exposed earth below,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:50.320
desperately burrowing through and only stopping to&nbsp;
use any rocks she found to try and stop the itch.&nbsp;

00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:55.680
All she achieved was drawing more blood.
Her fingers touched something soft, but solid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.680 --> 00:15:00.320
and Cait brushed away to clear more of the&nbsp;
excess dirt, trying to uncover what it was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.320 --> 00:15:05.840
It felt like a long mass just beneath the ground,&nbsp;
with a second similar object parallel to it. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.840 --> 00:15:11.440
the further along with it she reached, the quicker&nbsp;
she realized what it was… a leg. Clearing more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:17.920
of the dirt higher up, Cait could feel more. A&nbsp;
stomach, ribs, shoulders… and a head. Reaching for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:22.720
her flashlight, buried under some of the dirt that&nbsp;
had been dug up, she pointed it at the thing she’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:29.120
found: the woman, the same one from the nightmare.
Something inside her chest moved, scratching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:34.080
sharply, causing Cait to cough. And once she&nbsp;
started, she couldn’t stop. It was like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:40.800
itching she’d felt on the outside had now worked&nbsp;
its way inside and it was spreading… no, moving.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:46.720
In fact, not just moving but crawling. Through her&nbsp;
lungs, burrowing down into her stomach and, at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.720 --> 00:15:53.280
same time, upwards. The clawing of tiny legs,&nbsp;
digging their way up her windpipe, blocking it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:58.240
Cait couldn’t breathe, she dropped to her knees&nbsp;
retching as she struggled for air, spluttering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:03.200
as she felt something move at the back of her&nbsp;
throat. That was when she heard the buzzing again.&nbsp;

00:16:03.200 --> 00:16:05.760
Locusts.
A swarm of them came spilling out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:10.800
of Cait’s mouth. She couldn’t scream, let alone&nbsp;
catch her breath. They filled the shed in seconds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:16.400
engulfing her entire body as more and more of the&nbsp;
desert bugs still came pouring from her mouth.&nbsp;

00:16:16.400 --> 00:16:20.720
Outside, the wind kicked up again, with&nbsp;
enough force to blow the door to the woodshed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:25.840
closed again, trapping Cait inside as the bugs&nbsp;
tore at her. There was a single word on the wooden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.840 --> 00:16:30.800
surface of the door, written in deep, black&nbsp;
charcoal. She’d missed it, the door had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:34.720
open when she found the shed.
The word was ‘Witch’.&nbsp;

00:16:34.720 --> 00:16:38.960
The SCP Foundation would later find this&nbsp;
cursed cabin, and would naturally lose a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.960 --> 00:16:43.520
number of D-Classes to a horrific form of&nbsp;
locust-based suffocation. While there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.520 --> 00:16:48.480
clues as to what exactly is going on in this&nbsp;
terrible place - from the malice of a long dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:53.920
sorceress to the lingering revenge of a woman&nbsp;
wrongly persecuted - definitive answers as to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.920 --> 00:17:00.480
the anomalous mechanisms of the shed known as&nbsp;
SCP-4595 still elude even the Foundation’s finest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.480 --> 00:17:05.680
minds. It’s a place so mysterious and dangerous&nbsp;
that the special containment procedures read:&nbsp;

00:17:05.680 --> 00:17:11.120
“For safety purposes, no personnel&nbsp;
are permitted to enter SCP-4595.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.120 --> 00:17:16.560
SCP-4595 should only be entered by authorized&nbsp;
testing personnel under the supervision of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:25.440
SCP-4595 research lead. Under no circumstances&nbsp;
is SCP-4595-A to be disturbed in any way.”&nbsp;

00:17:25.440 --> 00:17:29.920
So perhaps we’ll never truly know why a&nbsp;
humble wooden shed in Jasper, Indiana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:35.600
causes such horrifying effects for its victims.&nbsp;
The SCP Foundation thinks it’s safer to just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.600 --> 00:17:40.640
lock it away and forget about it, letting its&nbsp;
ancient wood and old bones rot in the dark.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.640 --> 00:17:45.520
But if you really are curious about the truth,&nbsp;
you can always take a walk down the side of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.520 --> 00:17:51.600
Patoka River and pay the Witch a visit. If&nbsp;
you’re lucky, you might even get to leave…&nbsp;

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:58.320
Now go and check out “SCP-4000 TABOO” and&nbsp;
“SCP-3333 TOWER” to learn more about some strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:03.280
landmarks and places that it's best to avoid next&nbsp;
time you go hiking through the woods on your own.

